# 2. We must demote several "ratifications"

The document says:

$$
\mathcal R_{\rm req}\text{ v1.0 is RATIFIED}
$$

and later says the contradiction and EVal specifications are ratified. 

Given what we subsequently discovered in the corpus, I would **not treat those historical sign-off statements as evidence that the mathematics was actually ratified**.

For example, the document says:

$$
Contr(p)\iff S^+(p)>0\land S^-(p)>0
$$

and then introduces a threshold version:

$$
S^+(p)>\tau\land S^-(p)>\tau.
$$



But the threshold semantics were never independently established.

Similarly, it claims:

$$
EVal=
Standing\times Boundary\times Reason\times Context\times Provenance
$$

as a five-dimensional semantic structure. 

Our later D4 work correctly changed the interpretation:

$$
EVal_{\min}=S/\sim_{EVal}
$$

and asked whether that quotient **factorizes** into those dimensions.

That is a much stronger mathematical position.

So I would preserve the September 2 document as **historical research evidence**, but not use its “RATIFIED” labels as present-day mathematical authority.

