# 5. I would now define the Kernel differently from the September document

The candidate should be:

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal K^{cand}
=
(\mathfrak E_{\mathcal K},
\delta_{\mathcal K},
Inv_{\mathcal K},
\equiv_{\mathcal K})
}
$$

where:

### 1. Admissible state space

$$
\mathfrak E_{\mathcal K}
$$

is the set of states satisfying Kernel-owned invariants.

### 2. Transition mechanism

$$
\delta_{\mathcal K}:
\mathfrak E_{\mathcal K}
\times O
\times\Gamma
\rightharpoonup
\mathfrak E_{\mathcal K}.
$$

### 3. Kernel invariants

$$
Inv_{\mathcal K}
$$

are the properties that **must never be violated** by Kernel-governed transitions.

### 4. Identity/equivalence boundary

$$
\equiv_{\mathcal K}
$$

defines when two internal representations are indistinguishable with respect to the Kernel's obligations.

This is substantially cleaner than saying:

$$
Kernel=(A,R,E,\ldots).
$$

